<!-- ================================================================
     FIM Anomaly Detection Rules
     Kislley Rodrigues (m0us3r) - Wazuh Ambassador Program
     Reference Version: Wazuh Server 4.14.8 (serie 4.14.x)
     File: /var/ossec/etc/rules/fim_anomaly_rules.xml

     Base rules referenced (0015-ossec_rules.xml):
       550 (level 7) - syscheck_integrity_changed - group: syscheck_file
       553 (level 7) - syscheck_deleted           - group: syscheck_file
       554 (level 5) - syscheck_new_entry         - group: syscheck_file

     Detection scenarios (FIM-only, no Sysmon dependency):
       Scenario 1 - Suspicious file creation    (100300, 100301)
         Helper    - Counter isolation proxy    (100310, level 5)
       Scenario 2 - Suspicious file modification (100302)
       Scenario 3 - Suspicious file deletion     (100303)
       Escalation  - Combined mixed activity     (100304)

     LAB FINDINGS (Wazuh Server 4.14.8 - validated Oct 9-10, 2026)
     ================================================================
     [1] same_field in correlated rules maps to the decoded field name
         as exposed by the analysisd decoder. For FIM events, the
         correct field is "md5_after" (not "md5"). Confirmed by
         comparing alert JSON in the Wazuh Indexer against decoder output.

     [2] if_matched_sid with multiple comma-separated values is NOT
         reliably supported in correlated rules in Wazuh 4.14.8.
         Use if_matched_group on a shared group instead (see rule 100304).

     [3] Rule 100301 counter interference - RESOLVED (Oct 9, 2026):
         When 100300 and 100301 both use if_matched_sid 554, the
         analysisd counter for sid 554 is shared, causing 100300 to
         interfere with 100301 accumulation at event #3. Fix: helper
         rule 100310 (level 5) proxies every 554 event into groups
         fim_creation and fim_new_entry (see [6]). Rule 100301 now
         correlates on fim_new_entry, maintaining an independent counter
         isolated from 100300.

     [4] wazuh-logtest does NOT process syscheck events through Phase 2/3.
         The syscheck decoders are internal to the analysisd pipeline.
         FIM rules can only be validated through the real agent pipeline.

     [5] Counter interference confirmed on Linux (labnix, Oct 9, 2026):
         LAB FINDING [3] originally noted Windows (LABDESK) as pending.
         Testing on labnix confirmed the same interference occurs on Linux
         when 100300 and 100301 share if_matched_sid 554. The helper rule
         approach (100310 + if_matched_group fim_new_entry) is the fix
         applied to both platforms.

     [6] Rule 100300 child-rule interference - RESOLVED (Oct 9, 2026):
         Rule 100310 (if_sid 554, level 5) is a child rule that fires
         for every 554 event. Its presence as a child of 554 interferes
         with 100300's if_matched_sid 554 correlated counter - the child
         rule event is processed between 554 events, disrupting counter
         accumulation. Evidence: rule 100303 (if_matched_sid 553, no
         child rule) fires correctly; rule 100300 (if_matched_sid 554,
         child rule 100310 exists) does not. Fix: 100310 now emits to
         both fim_new_entry (for 100301) and fim_creation (for 100300).
         Rule 100300 changed from if_matched_sid 554 to
         if_matched_group fim_creation, giving it an independent counter
         isolated from the fim_new_entry counter used by 100301.

     [7] Rule 100310 level and 554 suppression - VALIDATED (Oct 10, 2026):
         When rule 100310 (if_sid 554) runs at level 5, analysisd
         suppresses the parent alert (rule 554). Only 100310 reaches
         wazuh-alerts-* and the OpenSearch index. Rule 554 appears
         only in wazuh-archives-* without a rule field.
         This is expected Wazuh behavior: child rule at level > 0
         consumes the parent event. Rule 100310 at level 5 is INTENTIONAL
         - it carries all syscheck.* fields identical to 554 and is
         the visible file-creation event in the pipeline.
         The FIM Alert Investigation panel (logic_fim.py) includes
         rule 100310 in FIM_FILE_RULES to handle this correctly.
         Reference: 100310 description explicitly states [base: rule 554].

     THRESHOLD NOTICE
     frequency="3" and timeframe="600" (10 minutes) are calibrated
     for lab testing only. In production, tune against a baseline of
     normal activity per agent group before deploying. The ignore="30"
     cooldown is also a lab value; set ignore="0" in production for
     full coverage.

     Deploy:
       chown wazuh:wazuh fim_anomaly_rules.xml
       chmod 660 fim_anomaly_rules.xml
       cp fim_anomaly_rules.xml /var/ossec/etc/rules/
       /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-analysisd -t 2>&1 | grep -i error
       systemctl restart wazuh-manager
     ================================================================ -->

<group name="syscheck,fim_anomaly,">

  <!-- ==============================================================
       SCENARIO 1 - Suspicious File Creation Activity
       Base SID: 554 | decoded_as: syscheck_new_entry
       Helper: rule 100310 (level 5) proxies every 554 event into
               groups fim_creation (for 100300) and fim_new_entry
               (for 100301), giving each rule an independent counter.
       See LAB FINDINGS [3], [5], [6], [7].
       ============================================================== -->

  <!-- HELPER | Dual counter isolation proxy - rules 100300 and 100301
       Level 5: generates a visible alert in wazuh-alerts-* carrying
       all syscheck.* fields from the parent 554 event. When this child
       rule fires, analysisd suppresses the parent 554 alert (LAB
       FINDING [7]). Rule 100310 IS the file-creation event in the
       pipeline. The FIM Alert Investigation panel (logic_fim.py)
       includes 100310 in FIM_FILE_RULES to handle this correctly.
       Counter isolation: every 554 event is forwarded into groups
       fim_creation (for 100300) and fim_new_entry (for 100301).
       Both counters are fully independent. Emitting to a single group
       per correlated rule prevents the child-rule interference
       described in LAB FINDING [6].                                  -->
  <rule id="100310" level="5">
    <if_sid>554</if_sid>
    <description>FIM: New file detected (syscheck_new_entry) [base: rule 554]. Event forwarded to fim_creation and fim_new_entry groups for independent counter isolation of rules 100300 and 100301.</description>
    <group>fim_creation,fim_new_entry,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 1A | Mass file creation on the same agent
       3+ file creation events within 10 minutes on the same agent.
       Correlates on fim_creation (via helper rule 100310) to avoid
       child-rule counter interference with if_matched_sid 554.
       See LAB FINDING [6].
       For multi-path setups, scope per directory using:
         <field name="file" type="pcre2">(?i)/target/path</field>
       Lab validation: confirmed on LABDESK (Windows) and labnix (Linux).
       MITRE: T1565.001, T1074                                         -->
  <rule id="100300" level="10" frequency="3" timeframe="600" ignore="30">
    <if_matched_group>fim_creation</if_matched_group>
    <description>FIM: 3 or more files created within 10 minutes. Possible malware staging or automated write activity. Lab threshold - tune for production.</description>
    <group>suspicious_creation,</group>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1565</id>
      <id>T1565.001</id>
      <id>T1074</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- 1B | Same file propagated across different directories
       Same MD5 hash (field: md5_after) detected in 3+ different
       paths within 10 minutes. Indicates dropper replication or
       lateral binary staging across the filesystem.
       Requires whodata or realtime for reliable hash population.
       Correlates on fim_new_entry (via helper rule 100310) to keep
       its counter independent from 100300's fim_creation counter.
       See LAB FINDINGS [3], [5], [6].
       Lab validation: confirmed on pentlab (Linux). Not confirmed on Windows
       (same_field over syscheck fields).
       MITRE: T1105, T1570                                             -->
  <rule id="100301" level="12" frequency="3" timeframe="600" ignore="30">
    <if_matched_group>fim_new_entry</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>md5_after</same_field>
    <different_field>file</different_field>
    <description>FIM: Same file hash created in 3 or more different directories within 10 minutes. Possible malware propagation or dropper replication. Lab threshold - tune for production.</description>
    <group>suspicious_creation,</group>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1105</id>
      <id>T1570</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- ==============================================================
       SCENARIO 2 - Suspicious File Modification Activity
       Base SID: 550 | decoded_as: syscheck_integrity_changed
       ============================================================== -->

  <!-- 2A | Mass file modification on the same agent
       3+ checksum-changed events within 10 minutes. Covers both
       same-directory and cross-directory patterns. Possible indicators:
       payload execution, in-place encryption, or config tampering.
       Lab validation: confirmed on LABDESK (Windows) and labnix (Linux).
       MITRE: T1565.001                                                -->
  <rule id="100302" level="10" frequency="3" timeframe="600" ignore="30">
    <if_matched_sid>550</if_matched_sid>
    <description>FIM: 3 or more files modified within 10 minutes. Possible payload execution, in-place encryption, or automated tampering. Lab threshold - tune for production.</description>
    <group>suspicious_modification,</group>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1565</id>
      <id>T1565.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- ==============================================================
       SCENARIO 3 - Suspicious File Deletion Activity
       Base SID: 553 | decoded_as: syscheck_deleted
       ============================================================== -->

  <!-- 3A | Mass file deletion on the same agent
       3+ file deletion events within 10 minutes. Indicators:
       evidence destruction, anti-forensics cleanup, log wiping,
       or malware removing artifacts post-execution.
       Lab validation: confirmed on LABDESK (Windows) and labnix (Linux).
       MITRE: T1485, T1070, T1561                                      -->
  <rule id="100303" level="10" frequency="3" timeframe="600" ignore="30">
    <if_matched_sid>553</if_matched_sid>
    <description>FIM: 3 or more files deleted within 10 minutes. Possible evidence destruction, anti-forensics, or post-execution malware cleanup. Lab threshold - tune for production.</description>
    <group>suspicious_deletion,</group>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1485</id>
      <id>T1070</id>
      <id>T1561</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- ==============================================================
       ESCALATION - Combined Mixed File Activity (level 14)
       Correlation via group: syscheck_file
       NOTE: if_matched_sid with multiple comma-separated values is
       NOT reliably supported in Wazuh 4.14.8 for correlated rules.
       if_matched_group on syscheck_file is the validated approach.
       Lab validation: confirmed on LABDESK (Windows) and labnix (Linux).
       ============================================================== -->
  <rule id="100304" level="14" frequency="5" timeframe="600" ignore="30">
    <if_matched_group>syscheck_file</if_matched_group>
    <description>FIM: High volume mixed file activity - 5 or more create, modify, and delete events within 10 minutes. Combined pattern suggesting active malware execution. Lab threshold - tune for production.</description>
    <group>suspicious_combined,fim_escalation,</group>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1485</id>
      <id>T1486</id>
      <id>T1565</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
